>  For spoken language we need to pause in suitable places.  For eSpeak
>  English, this includes at commas, brackets, quotation marks, and before
>  some words (usually conjunctions).  Also some common function words
>  should be unstressed so that the speech flows in a more natural way.
yes in lojban only the period is a pause. and it has it's own pecular
stress rules.
a comma can be used to show syllable boundaries. brackets, qoutes,etc
have words not pauses associated with them.

>
>  Lojban doesn't use commas, but it should be possible to identify
>  clauses, and therefore put pauses in the appropriate places.
>  The rule for "gi'" + vowel is an attempt to identify an end-of-clause.

yes that might be helpful, even if the lojban rules don't stricly need
them. I will ask what the opinion of the lojban experts are on that.


